    "evidence_text": "Epigenetic Control of Stem Cell Potential during Homeostasis, Aging, and Disease.\n\nBACKGROUND Age-associated epigenetic changes are implicated in aging.\n\nGenes harbouring hypomethylated enhancers were enriched for genes that change expression with age.\n\nThe role of epigenetics in aging and age-related diseases\n\nHere we discuss current knowledge about these matters and future directions in the field.\n\n DNA methylation and healthy human aging\n\nWidespread and tissue specific age-related DNA methylation changes in mice.\n\nIt is not known whether this age-related DNA methylation phenomenon is specific to human tissues.\n\nWe conclude that epigenetic deregulation is a common feature of aging in mammals.\n\nThe Aging Epigenome.\n\nExactly when and how this decline occurs is unknown.\n\nChanges in transcriptional networks and chromatin state lie at the heart of age-dependent decline.\n\nEpigenetic mechanisms in neurological disease\n\nThe role of epigenetics in aging and autoimmunity.\n\nLamin A-dependent nuclear defects in human aging.\n\nWhether lamin A plays any role in normal aging is unknown.\n\nWe show that the same molecular mechanism responsible for HGPS is active in healthy cells.\n\nInhibition of this splice site reverses the nuclear defects associated with aging.\n\nThese observations implicate lamin A in physiological aging.",
